
const KNOWLEDGE ="IT PROCUREMENT FOUNDATIONS — COURSE REFRESHER KNOWLEDGE\nSource: validated 80-slide course deck, 20 October 2026.\n\n=== TOPIC BLOCKS START ===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_01\nTOPIC_NAME: Software Inflation\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 8, 9, 10\n\n2. Core ideas:\n• Software inflation is the progressive growth of an organization's software estate—in terms of applications, subscriptions, licenses, and consumption-based services—leading to rising costs, increasing complexity, and diminishing cost efficiency.\n• Key drivers:\n  o Price inflation\n  o Volume inflation\n  o Add-on inflation (AI features, premium modules)\n  o Portfolio inflation (new applications)\n  o Consumption inflation (tokens, APIs, storage, compute)\n3. Blog:\nhttps://www.goodspending.com/post/microsoft-ai-licensing-understanding-through-practice\n4. External:\nhttps://www.vertice.one/l/saas-inflation-index-report\n5. Practical value:\n• Review software adoption regularly.\n• Measure realized benefits separately from promised value.\n• Segment licenses by role.\n• Use trials before large deployments.\n• Apply quotas and consumption caps where appropriate.\n• Manage change and user adoption.\n\nEXAMPLES:\n• Microsoft 365 Copilot rollout\n• AI add-on licensing\n• Consumption-based AI services\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_03 — SaaS Market Reality & Trends; TOPIC_16 — The SaaSpocalypse; TOPIC_13 — Cloud Services\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_02\nTOPIC_NAME: Digitalization Paradox\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 15\n\n2. Core ideas:\nTechnology ≠ Transformation\nTransformation without adoption and measurable outcomes is likely a waste of funds.\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/08/digital-transformation-diminishing-returns.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.distributed.com/blog/gartner-says-80-of-it-projects-fail-but-not-with-distributed-s-elastic-teams\n5. Practical value:\nApply the full P.O.W.E.R. model\nDon’t buy technology as a solution (don’t start with E in P.O.W.E.R.)\nLink contracts to adoption & outcomes\nUse POC → scale (incremental development and financing)\nAvoid “promised value” – separate legacy from innovation; measure outcomes separately.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nERP implemented → old processes maintained through customization → no value\nAI deployed → no adoption → no usage\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: None explicitly identified in the source topic block.\nASSOCIATED_CONCEPTS_FROM_SOURCE: P.O.W.E.R. model of digital transformation — Processes; Operating model; Workforce (People); Enablement; Results\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_03\nTOPIC_NAME: SaaS Market Reality & Trends\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 7, 11, 44, 45, 47, 48, 49, 50, 51, 52\n\n2. Core ideas:\nSaaS growth ≠ SaaS efficiency\nHigh software gross margins can coexist with low operating margins or losses after S&M, R&D and G&A. Assess supplier-specific economics before inferring discount capacity.\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/10/the-saas-market-and-it-procurement-350.html\n4. External:\nhttps://zylo.com/reports/2026-saas-management-index/\nhttps://www.vertice.one/l/saas-inflation-index-report\nhttps://www.highalpha.com/saas-benchmarks\n5. Practical value:\nTreat SaaS as a portfolio\nControl software inflation\nEliminate:\nshelfware\nredundant apps\nmulti-channel spend\nshadow IT spend\nChallenge:\nauto-renewals\nhidden pricing\nunbudgeted/unapproved spend\nShift:\nEvaluate seat, consumption and outcome-linked charging. Outcome-based pricing is a subset of value-based pricing, not a separate stage beyond it.\n\nEXAMPLES:\n275 apps per company → scope overlap → waste\nAuto-renewal +9% → uncontrolled software inflation\nAI hype → bundle and upsell → budget overrun\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_02 — Digitalization Paradox; TOPIC_16 — The SaaSpocalypse; TOPIC_01 — Software Inflation\nASSOCIATED_CONCEPTS_FROM_SOURCE: SaaS Vendor Economics; AI vs SaaS\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_04\nTOPIC_NAME: Servitization of IT\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 14\n\n2. Core ideas:\nIT shifts from product ownership to service consumption\nValue is delivered through relationships, not just assets\nServitization improves customer retention and enables new value streams.\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/03/value-procurement-framework.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/17/19/8756\n5. Practical value:\nShift focus from asset ownership to service performance\nEvaluate suppliers based on outcomes and SLAs\nReduce upfront investment, increase flexibility to scale up and down\nAlign sourcing with long-term service value\n\nEXAMPLES:\nAnything as a Service (XaaS)\nAI as a Service (AIaaS)\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_02 — Digitalization Paradox; TOPIC_11 — NIST Cloud Computing Model; TOPIC_03 — SaaS Market Reality & Trends\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_05\nTOPIC_NAME: Digitization vs Digitalization\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 15\n\n2. Core ideas:\nDigitization = converting analog information into digital form\nDigitalization = using digital technologies to improve or redesign processes\nDigitization is a technical step; digitalization is a business transformation\n3. Blog:\nN/A\n4. External:\nhttps://www.sap.com/resources/digitization-vs-digitalization\n5. Practical value:\nDo not confuse data conversion with transformation\nDigitization alone does not create value\nFocus on process change and adoption, not just systems\nUse digitization as an input to digitalization initiatives\n\nEXAMPLES:\nScanning paper contracts → digitization\nImplementing workflow automation on top → digitalization\nConverting invoices to PDF → digitization\nAutomating invoice approval and matching → digitalization\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_02 — Digitalization Paradox\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_06\nTOPIC_NAME: Bimodal IT\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 17, 18, 19\n\n2. Core ideas:\nIT operates in two parallel modes:\n – Mode 1: stability, reliability, efficiency\n – Mode 2: agility, experimentation, innovation\nThe model separates core systems from innovation initiatives\nDesigned to balance risk control vs speed of change\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2021/04/bimodal-procurement-vs-adaptive-sourcing.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.gartner.com/en/information-technology/glossary/bimodal\n5. Practical value:\nDo not apply generic governance to all IT initiatives\nSeparate sourcing strategies:\nMode 1 → long-term, stable, cost-optimized contracts\nMode 2 → flexible, short-term, experimental sourcing\nAlign procurement with:\nrisk tolerance\ncontract lifespan and supplier relationships\nbusiness impact\n\nEXAMPLES:\nERP system → Mode 1 (stability, control) → long-term contract with a strategic supplier\nAI pilot / analytics tool → Mode 2 (experimentation, speed) → short-term contract based on the POC outcome with a no-name supplier\nCore infrastructure outsourcing → Mode 1\nStartup SaaS tool trial → Mode 2\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_08 — Pace Layer Model; TOPIC_07 — Buygrid Framework\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_07\nTOPIC_NAME: Buygrid Framework\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 20, 25\n\n2. Core ideas:\nThe Buygrid Framework explains how organizations make purchasing decisions\nIt combines:\nBuy phases (steps in the process)\nBuy classes (type of purchase situation)\nDecision complexity depends on:\nNovelty\nrisk\nvalue.\n3. Blog\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2022/06/the-new-look-on-industrial-buying.html\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2023/09/it-procurement-vs-legacy-sourcing.html\n4. External:\nhttps://faculty.wharton.upenn.edu/wp-content/uploads/2012/04/9802_The_BuyGrid_Model_30_Years.pdf\n5. Practical value:\nAdjust sourcing strategy based on buy class:\nNew Task → high effort, exploration, stakeholder alignment\nModified Rebuy → optimization, negotiation\nStraight Rebuy → efficiency, automation\nAvoid using a single approach for all purchases\n\nEXAMPLES:\nFirst-time ERP implementation → New Task\nSwitching cloud provider → Modified Rebuy\nRenewing SaaS licenses → Straight Rebuy\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_21 — Kraljic Matrix\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_08\nTOPIC_NAME: Pace Layer Model\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 21, 22, 23, 25\n\n2. Core ideas:\nIT systems evolve at different speeds and must be managed accordingly\nThree layers:\nSystems of Record → stable, core processes, data, and transactions\nSystems of Differentiation → support advanced business processes and competitive positioning, enable current competitive advantage\nSystems of Innovation → experimental, rapidly changing solutions, provide future competitive advantage.\nEach layer requires a different:\ngovernance model\nsourcing approach\ninvestment horizon\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2021/04/bimodal-procurement-vs-adaptive-sourcing.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.gartner.com/en/information-technology/glossary/pace-layer-application-strategy\nhttps://cio-wiki.org/wiki/Gartner%27s_PACE_Layered_Application_Strategy\n5. Practical value:\nDo not apply uniform sourcing across all systems\nAlign procurement strategy with system layer:\nSystems of Record → stability, long-term contracts, risk control\nSystems of Differentiation → flexibility, medium-term, best-of-breed “beauty contest”\nSystems of Innovation → speed, experimentation, short cycles\n\nEXAMPLES:\nERP / financial systems → Systems of Record\nCRM customization → Systems of Differentiation\nAI pilot tools → Systems of Innovation\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_16 — The SaaSpocalypse; TOPIC_21 — Kraljic Matrix\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_09\nTOPIC_NAME: ITIL Service Lifecycle\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 26, 54\n\n2. Core ideas:\nITIL defines how IT services are designed, delivered, and improved across their lifecycle. It starts from the Business/Customer requirements, demonstrating IT’s dedication to the business.\nFive lifecycle stages:\nService Strategy → define budget, demand, and service portfolio\nService Design → design services, processes, and SLAs\nService Transition → build, test, and deploy services\nService Operation → deliver and support services\nContinual Service Improvement (CSI) → measure and enhance performance\nFocus shifts from systems to end-to-end service value.\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2021/05/procurement-40-adaptivity-beyond-agility.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.axelos.com/best-practice-solutions/itil\n5. Practical value:\nProcurement must align with lifecycle stage:\nStrategy → define sourcing model, make/buy decisions\nDesign → embed SLAs, KPIs, and service levels in contracts\nTransition → manage vendor onboarding and risk\nOperation → monitor performance and enforce SLAs\nCSI → drive continuous improvement and renegotiation\nAvoid:\nfocusing only on purchase (design)\nignoring lifecycle costs, performance, and project rollout plan\n\nEXAMPLES:\nOutsourcing IT support:\nStrategy → define scope\nDesign → SLA structure\nTransition → migration\nOperation → service delivery\nCSI → performance improvement\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_04 — Servitization of IT; TOPIC_10 — TCO of a Technology Service\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_10\nTOPIC_NAME: TCO of a Technology Service\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 24, 29, 30, 54\n\n2. Core ideas:\nTCO is mapped across the ITIL Service Lifecycle\nCosts are not static — they occur across Strategy → Design → Transition → Operation → End-of-life\nTCO is a function of decisions, not a fixed number.\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/07/opportunity-cost-vs-tco-in-outsourcing.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.meegle.com/en_us/topics/it-service/tco-total-cost-of-ownership-in-it\n5. Practical value:\nTCO of a technology service may include:\nService Strategy\nCost of discovery (business analysis)\nMarket research\nSupplier evaluation\nPOC / MVP / prototype\nRelationship building\nNegotiations\nService Design\nCost of service design\nSupplier selection\nContract negotiation\nSupplier audit\nScoping\nService Transition\nSetup (cloud / infrastructure)\nCustomization\nHardware / software acquisition\nDeployment and migration\nTesting\nLegacy decommissioning\nProject management\nChange management\nKnowledge management\nService Operation\nCloud services\nSoftware rental\nNetwork costs\nChange requests (incl. renegotiation)\nSupport and maintenance:\nPreventative / corrective (SaaS)\nPerfective / adaptive (ad-hoc)\nUpgrades (software / firmware)\nAsset management\nSRM\nCompliance (technical, legal, environmental)\nRisk management\nEnd-of-life\nRetirement / replacement costs\nEnvironmental costs\n\nEXAMPLES:\nERP cost is not $80k–$120k, but ranges from $3k → $1M+ depending on a solution depth, scale, integrations, customization, teams, and decision-making.\nBritish Airways case:\n£27.5M savings vs £150M failure impact\nTCO considerations must include risk exposure and opportunity cost.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_09 — ITIL Service Lifecycle; TOPIC_04 — Servitization of IT; TOPIC_21 — Kraljic Matrix\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_11\nTOPIC_NAME: NIST Cloud Computing Model\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 32, 33\n\n2. Core ideas:\n“Cloud computing is a model for enabling ubiquitous, convenient, on-demand network access to a shared pool of configurable computing resources … that can be rapidly provisioned and released with minimal management effort or service provider interaction.”\n3. Blog\n4. External:\nhttps://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/legacy/sp/nistspecialpublication800-145.pdf\n5. Practical value:\nThe most neglected characteristics in practice:\non-demand self-service\nrapid elasticity\nProcurement implication:\nDo not assume “cloud” = compliant with NIST model\nValidate:\nability to scale down (not only up)\nreal usage-based charging\nindependence from provider intervention\n\nEXAMPLES:\nSaaS with no scale-down → violates elasticity → shelfware occurs.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_12 — Cloud Deployment Models; TOPIC_13 — Cloud Services\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_12\nTOPIC_NAME: Cloud Deployment Models\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 35\n\n2. Core ideas:\nCloud deployment defines where and how infrastructure is operated and accessed\nFour deployment models:\nPublic cloud\nprovider-owned infrastructure available to the general public\nPrivate cloud\nenvironment dedicated to a single organization\nHybrid cloud\ncombination of public/private integrated with on-premise systems\nCommunity cloud\nshared infrastructure used by multiple organizations with common requirements.\n3. Blog:\n4. External:\nhttps://www.flackbox.com/cloud-deployment-models-public-private-hybrid-cloud\n5. Practical value:\nDeployment choice impacts:\ndata protection\ncost structure\ncontrol vs flexibility\nPublic cloud:\nscalability, accessibility\nlower control and security but lower cost\nPrivate cloud:\nhigh control and security\nhigher cost\nHybrid cloud:\nbalance of flexibility and control\nintegration complexity\nCommunity cloud:\nshared compliance environment\ncost sharing across organizations with similar needs.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nPublic → SaaS platforms\nPrivate → internal regulated systems\nHybrid → sensitive data on-prem + scalable workloads in cloud\nCommunity → government / regulated sector shared infrastructure\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_11 — NIST Cloud Computing Model; TOPIC_13 — Cloud Services\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_13\nTOPIC_NAME: Cloud Services\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 34, 36\n\n2. Core ideas:\nCloud services are structured in three service models:\nIaaS (Infrastructure as a Service)\nbasic compute, storage, and network resources\nPaaS (Platform as a Service)\non-demand application-hosting environment\nSaaS (Software as a Service)\nready-to-use cloud applications\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/09/cost-and-performance-tradeoff-with-multiobjective-analysis.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.redhat.com/en/topics/cloud-computing/what-are-cloud-services\n5. Practical value:\nService model determines:\nlevel of control\nresponsibility split\ncost structure\nIaaS:\nmaximum control\nhigher internal management effort\nPaaS:\nreduced infrastructure management\nfocus on application development\nSaaS:\nminimal management effort\nfastest deployment\nKey sourcing implication:\nThe higher the service layer → the less control, but the faster the deployment\n\nEXAMPLES:\nIaaS → virtual machines, storage\nPaaS → development platforms, databases\nSaaS → CRM, ERP cloud applications\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_11 — NIST Cloud Computing Model; TOPIC_12 — Cloud Deployment Models; TOPIC_27 — Technology Procurement Framework\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_14\nTOPIC_NAME: Make or Buy\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 38, 39\n\n2. Core ideas:\nMake or Buy is a governance decision.\nMake means the organisation delivers the activity through internal resources and management authority.\nBuy means an independent supplier delivers the activity under contract.\n\nThis is separate from the solution form:\n• COTS / SaaS — adopt a ready-made market solution\n• Customise — adapt an existing solution\n• Build — develop a new solution\n\nA supplier can build a custom solution, so Build does not automatically mean Make. An organisation can also Make the service while using purchased COTS components.\n\nAfter selecting governance and solution form, choose a delivery model. Waterfall, incremental and agile describe how the solution is delivered; they do not replace the Make or Buy decision.\n\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2023/12/modern-dynamics-of-saas-procurement-negotiations.html\n\n4. External:\nN/A — use the Transaction Cost Economics references in TOPIC_28 for the governance decision.\n\n5. Practical value:\nUse this sequence:\n1. Define the activity or capability being governed.\n2. Use Transaction Cost Economics to assess market, hybrid or hierarchy governance.\n3. Select Make or Buy.\n4. Select COTS / SaaS, customise or build as the solution form.\n5. Select the delivery model: waterfall, incremental or agile.\n\nEXAMPLES:\n• Buy + COTS: subscribe to a standard SaaS CRM.\n• Buy + Build: contract a software company to develop a custom application.\n• Make + Build: use an internal product team to create the application.\n• Make + COTS: operate an internally managed service using purchased software.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_28 — Transaction Cost Economics; TOPIC_12 — Cloud Deployment Models; TOPIC_13 — Cloud Services\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_15\nTOPIC_NAME: Horizontal, Vertical, and Nano-SaaS\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 43\n\n2. Core ideas:\nHorizontal SaaS\ngeneral-purpose solutions used across industries\nVertical SaaS\nindustry-specific solutions tailored to a particular sector\nNano / Micro SaaS\nultra-specialised applications solving a single narrow task\nStrategic positioning:\nHorizontal SaaS → broad cost leadership / scale\nVertical SaaS → differentiation / deep industry expertise\nNano-SaaS → micro-services / differentiation focus\n3. Blog:\nhttps://www.goodspending.com/post/nano-saas-your-personal-digital-tranformation\n4. External:\nhttps://flgpartners.com/saas-industry-centric-business-models-horizontal-vertical/\n5. Practical value:\nHorizontal SaaS:\nleverage competition\nbenchmark pricing\npush for modular and flexible pricing\nVertical SaaS:\njustify premium pricing via domain expertise\nsecure:\nintegration support\nuptime guarantees\nroadmap influence\nNano / Micro SaaS:\nfast adoption\nhigh fragmentation risk\ncontributes to:\nshadow IT/vibe coding\nredundant apps\n\nEXAMPLES:\nHorizontal → CRM, collaboration tools\nVertical → banking SaaS, healthcare SaaS\nNano → single-purpose tools (e.g. niche AI apps)\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_13 — Cloud Services; TOPIC_03 — SaaS Market Reality & Trends; TOPIC_16 — The SaaSpocalypse\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_16\nTOPIC_NAME: The SaaSpocalypse\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 44, 45, 47, 48, 49, 50, 51, 52\n\n2. Core ideas:\nSaaS market is shifting under pressure from:\nPricing model transformation\nconsumption-based\nvalue-based\nhybrid pricing models\nEconomic pressure on SaaS vendors:\nlow net profit margins (~1.5–2%)\nhigh customer acquisition cost\nlong payback period (~22 months)\nShift in revenue strategy:\nfocus on existing customers (retention & expansion)\nupsell hyped services (AI) to improve overall outlook\nAI as a threat:\nfight accelerates for data, control, and orchestration layer\nincreasing pressure from AI agents and vibe coding\n3. Blog:\nhttps://www.goodspending.com/post/from-telco-dumb-pipes-to-ai-orchestrated-software\n4. External:\nhttps://www.thesaascfo.com/the-saaspocalypse-ai-agents-vibe-coding-and-the-changing-economics-of-saas/\n5. Practical value:\nConsumption-based model\nsuited for variable demand\nrisk: uncontrolled cost growth\nValue-based model\nsuited for differentiated solutions\nrequires clear ROI linkage\nSubscription-based model\npredictable cost\nrisk of overcommitment\nKey SaaS negotiation logic:\nemphasize retention value\nanalyse CLTV (Customer Lifetime Value)\npush for benefits as a long-term customer\nalign pricing with actual usage and value delivery\ndon’t accept “promised value”\n\nEXAMPLES:\nVendor pushes consumption pricing → buyer must control usage\nLong-term contracts → leverage retention economics\nAI monetization → unclear value vs cost\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_03 — SaaS Market Reality & Trends\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_17\nTOPIC_NAME: SaaS Pricing Models\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 37, 40, 47, 78\n\n2. Core ideas:\n• Common pricing models include:\n  o Seat-based pricing\n  o Consumption-based pricing\n  o Value-based pricing\n  o Tiered pricing\n  o Flat-rate subscription\n• Each model allocates commercial risk differently between customer and supplier.\n3. Blog:\nN/A\n4. External:\nhttps://www.paddle.com/blog/saas-pricing-models-strategies-fltr\n5. Practical value:\n• Match the pricing model to demand predictability.\n• Seat-based pricing offers budget certainty but creates shelfware risk.\n• Consumption-based pricing improves flexibility but requires strong governance.\n• Value-based pricing aligns cost with business outcomes but requires measurable value.\n\nEXAMPLES:\n• Microsoft 365 – seat-based\n• Azure OpenAI – consumption-based\n• Procurement analytics platform priced on managed spend – value-based\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_03 — SaaS Market Reality & Trends; TOPIC_13 — Cloud Services; TOPIC_10 — TCO of a Technology Service; TOPIC_27 — Technology Procurement Framework\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_18\nTOPIC_NAME: Types of IT Outsourcing Services\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 57\n\n2. Core ideas:\nThere are at least four main outsourcing models:\nStaff Augmentation\ntemporary resources to cover skill or capacity gaps\nIT Outsourcing (end-to-end)\noutsourcing of complete teams, functions, or critical services\nManaged Services\ninfrastructure-centric outsourcing of highly complex business processes or critical services\nAs-a-Service\nstandardized, cloud-based, application-centric services.\nKey dimensions across models:\nControl → highest in staff augmentation, lowest in as-a-service\nSpeed (to mobilize) → fastest in as-a-service, slowest in large outsourcing\nContract type →\nrate card / T&M (time & material)\nproject fee / retainer\nSLA-based long-term agreements\nsubscription agreements\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2021/04/deep-dive-into-outsourcing.html\n4. External:\nhttps://kruschecompany.com/it-outsourcing-types/\n5. Practical value:\nSelection depends on:\nurgency of need\ncomplexity of service\nrequired control\nstrategic importance\nTypical use cases:\nStaff augmentation:\nimmediate resource gaps\nManaged services:\noutsourcing defined operational processes\nIT outsourcing:\ncomplex or critical services\nlong-term strategic delivery\nAs-a-Service:\nstandardized, automated cloud solutions\nCritical limitation of outsourcing:\nDo NOT outsource activities that define competitive advantage, critical data, or core decision-making\n\nEXAMPLES:\nStaff augmentation → hiring developers via T&M\nIT outsourcing → outsourced IT support\nManaged services → data transmission network operation and maintenance\nAs-a-Service → SaaS / cloud-based platforms (e.g., SAP Concur)\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_04 — Servitization of IT; TOPIC_10 — TCO of a Technology Service\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_19\nTOPIC_NAME: Outsourcing Delivery Models\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 59\n\n2. Core ideas:\nDelivery models define where and how services are delivered geographically\nMain models:\nOnsite / Landed\nresources located at the client’s premises\nOnshore\ndelivery within the same country\nNearshore\ndelivery from a neighbouring or nearby country\nOffshore\ndelivery from a distant country (typically low-cost locations)\nCore trade-off:\nCost vs control vs proximity\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2021/04/deep-dive-into-outsourcing.html\n4. External:\nhttps://orases.com/blog/the-differences-between-onshore-offshore-software-development/\n5. Practical value:\nOnsite / Landed:\nhighest cost\nhighest control and communication quality\nOnshore:\nhigh cost\nstrong alignment with business and regulation\nNearshore:\nbalanced cost\nmanageable time zone and cultural alignment\nOffshore:\nlowest cost\nhighest coordination complexity\nlower direct control\nCritical sourcing implication:\nallocate activities based on value:\nhigh-value / strategic → onsite / onshore\nexecution / scalable tasks → nearshore / offshore\n\nEXAMPLES:\nOnsite → project management, architecture\nNearshore → development teams\nOffshore → testing, support (L1/L2/L3)\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_18 — Types of IT Outsourcing Services; TOPIC_04 — Servitization of IT; TOPIC_10 — TCO of a Technology Service\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_20\nTOPIC_NAME: ISO 19770-1:2017 Framework of ITAM Processes\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 63, 64\n\n2. Core ideas:\nIT Asset Management (ITAM) is structured as a management system aligned to lifecycle and governance\nThree core pillars of ITAM:\n1 – Trustworthy Data\naccurate, complete, and reliable asset data\n2 – Lifecycle Integration\nassets managed across full lifecycle: Plan → Acquire/Develop → Maintain → Retire\n3 – Optimization\ncontinuous improvement and cost/value optimization.\nManagement system structure (PDCA cycle):\nPlan → Do → Check → Act\nembedded across ITAM processes\nCore process areas:\nContract management\nFinancial management\nVendor management\nLicense management\nSecurity management\nData management\nChange management\nService level & risk management\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/01/5-solutions-to-increase-saas-savings-in-procurement.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.iso.org/standard/68531.html\n5. Practical value:\nITAM is a foundation for IT procurement control:\nenables:\ncost savings\ncost avoidance\ncompliance\ncontrol\nProcurement implication:\nWithout ITAM → no visibility → no control over licenses, assets, and spend\n\nEXAMPLES:\nLicense audit (SAP / Oracle) → requires accurate usage data\nAsset lifecycle tracking → prevents over-purchasing\nVendor management integration → aligns contracts with actual usage\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_10 — TCO of a Technology Service\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_21\nTOPIC_NAME: Kraljic Matrix\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 66\n\n2. Core ideas:\nThe Kraljic Matrix classifies purchases based on:\nProfit impact (value / business importance)\nSupply risk (market complexity / level of competition/ availability of goods/services)\nFour Quadrants:\nNon-critical items\nlow value, low risk\nLeverage items\nhigh value, low risk\nBottleneck items\nlow value, high risk\nStrategic items\nhigh value, high risk\nTypical sourcing strategies:\nNon-critical:\nstandardize\nconsolidate\nefficient buying channels\nLeverage:\nexploit competition\nnegotiate aggressively\nuse market power\nBottleneck:\nsecure supply\nreduce dependency\nmanage risk\nStrategic:\nlong-term partnerships\nperformance incentives\njoint planning (e.g., CPFR)\nBlog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/09/game-theory-and-procurement-strategic.html\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2023/01/adding-new-dimensions-to-kraljic-matrix.html\n4. External:\nhttps://hbr.org/1983/09/purchasing-must-become-supply-management\n5. Practical value:\nDo NOT apply a single sourcing strategy to all categories\nAlign procurement approach with:\nsupply market complexity\nbusiness impact\nIT-specific implication:\nStrategic → ERP, core platforms\nLeverage → standard software, hardware\nBottleneck → niche technologies\nNon-critical → routine IT commodities.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nERP system → Strategic\nsoftware licenses with many vendors → Leverage\nniche cybersecurity tool → Bottleneck\nstandard hardware → Non-critical.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_07 — Buygrid Framework\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_22\nTOPIC_NAME: Sourcing Value Levers\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 28, 62, 67\n\n2. Core ideas:\nValue levers are structured ways to create value in sourcing\nThey operate across multiple dimensions of a deal, not only price\nGroups of core value levers:\nDemand management\nSpecification / scope optimisation\nCommercial model design\nSupplier competition / selection\nContract structure and terms\nPerformance management\nSupplier relationship management\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2024/01/saas-negotiation-strategies.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.kearney.com/insights/books/the-purchasing-chessboard/levers\n5. Practical value:\nValue is created by designing the sourcing process, not just negotiating\nKey principle: Each lever targets a different part of the value chain.\nWhat’s you permission to buy\nwhat you buy vs. what you need\nhow you buy\nfrom whom\nunder what terms\nhow it performs\nCritical implication:\nPrice = only one of many sourcing value levers\nOver-focusing on price → value destruction\n\nEXAMPLES:\nDemand management → reduce SaaS license consumption\nCommercial model → shift to consumption pricing\nContract → introduce true-up/down\nPerformance → SLA + service credits\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_10 — TCO of a Technology Service; TOPIC_21 — Kraljic Matrix; TOPIC_07 — Buygrid Framework; TOPIC_27 — Technology Procurement Framework\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_23\nTOPIC_NAME: Information Asymmetry\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 65, 68, 69, 70\n\n2. Core ideas:\nInformation asymmetry occurs when one party possesses more or better information than the other\nIn procurement, suppliers often know more about:\ncosts\ntechnical complexity\nimplementation risks\nservice capability\nInformation asymmetry creates sourcing and governance risks across the contract lifecycle\n\nClassic forms of information asymmetry:\nAdverse selection (BEFORE THE DEAL)\nsupplier overstates its capability before contract award\nbuyer cannot fully distinguish between good and bad suppliers\nMoral hazard (AFTER THE DEAL)\nsupplier behavior deteriorates after contract award\nhidden actions and reduced effort become difficult to observe\nPrincipal-agent problem\no agent incentives diverge from the principal objectives\no advisors or integrators may optimize their own interests instead of the buyer value\n3. Blog\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2023/10/information-asymmetry-in-saas-negotiations.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/economics-econometrics-and-finance/information-asymmetry\n5. Practical value:\nConduct supplier due diligence\nValidate supplier claims through references and benchmarking\nUse:\nKPIs\nSLAs\ngovernance structures\noutcome-based contracts\nReduce dependency on opaque supplier-controlled information\nAlign incentives to reduce opportunistic behavior.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nSupplier intentionally “low-balls” implementation costs during tender\nSaaS vendor hides future scaling and support costs\nIntegrator maximizes billable hours instead of long-term customer value\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_25 — Incomplete Contracts; TOPIC_26 — Relational Contracts; TOPIC_24 — Types of Negotiations and Game Theory\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_24\nTOPIC_NAME: Types of Negotiations and Game Theory\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 53, 65, 69, 71\n\n2. Core ideas:\nTwo fundamental types of negotiations:\nDistributive negotiations (= fixed pie, win-lose, zero-sum games)\nFocus: claiming value\nOne party’s gain = other party’s loss\nNo expansion of total value\nIntegrative negotiations (= win-win, cooperative games)\nFocus: creating value\nExpand the “pie” before dividing it\nMutual benefit possible\n3. Blog:\nhttps://www.goodspending.com/post/integrative-and-distributive-negotiations\n4. External:\nhttps://www.pon.harvard.edu/daily/negotiation-skills-daily/negotiation-skills-expanding-the-pie-integrative-bargaining-versus-distributive-bargaining/\n5. Practical value:\nIntegrative (cooperative) tactics:\nInterest exploration\nInformation sharing\nTrust building\nJoint problem-solving\nCreating multiple options\nDistributive (zero-sum) tactics:\nAggressive anchoring\nClaiming value\nWithholding information\nUsing leverage\nMapping to Kraljic:\nLeverage / Non-critical → Distributive\nStrategic / Bottleneck → Integrative\n\nEXAMPLES:\nSaaS renewal with many vendors → distributive (price pressure)\nERP transformation → integrative (value + risk sharing)\nGame theory\nNegotiation = strategic interaction between players\nOutcomes depend on:\nyour actions\ncounterparty actions\nNash Equilibrium→ no party benefits from changing strategy unilaterally\nExample:\nConsultant chooses between:\nbest supplier (value for company)\nsuboptimal supplier (personal gain)\nSolution:\nincentive-based contract aligns interests\nequilibrium achieved when both maximise payoff\nNegotiation is a game:\nDistributive = fight for value\nIntegrative = create value\nGame theory = design the rules so the “right” outcome becomes rational.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_23 — Information Asymmetry; TOPIC_21 — Kraljic Matrix; TOPIC_22 — Sourcing Value Levers\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_25\nTOPIC_NAME: Incomplete Contracts\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 72, 75, 76, 77, 79\n\n2. Core ideas:\n“It is typically impossible to write a contract that explicitly specifies what each party should do in every possible future state of the world.”\nContracts are inherently incomplete because:\nfuture events cannot be fully predicted\nnot all contingencies can be specified\ndecision rights must be allocated\nConsequence:\ncontracts leave gaps\nthese gaps create room for renegotiation\nand opportunistic behaviour (“self-interest with guile”)\nHold-up problem:\noccurs when one party makes relationship-specific investment\nthe counterparty can then:\nexploit dependency\nrenegotiate terms\ncapture additional value\nResult:\nunderinvestment\ninefficiency\nrisk of value destruction\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2021/06/relational-governance-in-outsourcing-contracts.html\n4. External:\nhttps://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/hart-lecture.pdf\n5. Practical value:\nYou cannot “fix” incompleteness → you must design for it\nKey procurement implications:\ndefine decision rights clearly\nuse:\ngovernance mechanisms\nescalation paths\nchange management clauses\nmitigate hold-up risk:\navoid over-dependence\ndiversify suppliers where possible\nstructure contracts to balance power\nalign incentives → reduce motivation for opportunistic behaviour\n\nEXAMPLES:\nBuyer invests in specialized equipment\nSupplier later raises prices → exploits dependency\nResult as per the Game Theory:\nbuyer cannot recover the investment\nsupplier may capture short-term additional value\nboth parties suffer from anyone’s opportunistic behaviour.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_23 — Information Asymmetry; TOPIC_24 — Types of Negotiations and Game Theory; TOPIC_22 — Sourcing Value Levers\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_26\nTOPIC_NAME: Relational Contracts\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 73, 74, 75, 76, 77, 79\n\n2. Core ideas:\nContracts exist on a spectrum:\nDiscrete (formal) contracts\nRelational contracts\nRelational contracts:\nbased on trust, cooperation, and long-term relationship\nnot fully specified in formal terms\nrely on mutual understanding and ongoing interaction\nKey contrast:\nFormal contracts → “fixed everything”\nRelational contracts → “shared risk”\nApplicable where:\nuncertainty is high\noutcomes cannot be fully defined upfront\ncontinuous adaptation is required\n3. Blog:\nhttps://ukrsedo.blogspot.com/2021/06/relational-governance-in-outsourcing-contracts.html\n4. External:\nhttps://golab.bsg.ox.ac.uk/the-basics/relational-contracting/\n5. Practical value:\nRelational contracts are a response to incomplete contracts\nUse when:\nhigh complexity\nlong-term dependency\ninnovation / transformation scope\nKey mechanisms:\nshared objectives\nflexibility in execution\ncontinuous negotiation\ngovernance instead of strict control\nCritical implication:\nYou cannot manage complex IT services with purely rigid (formal) contracts\nProcurement role is to build relationship + governance, not just legal structure\n\nEXAMPLES:\nComplex IT outsourcing\nrequires ongoing adjustments\nInnovation / transformation program\ncannot be fully specified upfront\nRelational contracts manage uncertainty through trust and governance — not through completeness.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_25 — Incomplete Contracts; TOPIC_24 — Types of Negotiations and Game Theory\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_27\nTOPIC_NAME: Technology Procurement Framework\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 16, 34, 37, 45\n\n2. Core ideas:\nThe Technology Procurement Framework separates procurement cost action into two sequential levels:\n\nLevel 1 — Demand Management\nDemand must be understood and challenged before commercial negotiation.\n\nWithin Demand Management:\n• Cost Management — make demand, usage, ownership and spend visible and controllable.\n• Cost Optimization — remove unnecessary demand and improve the efficiency of the remaining requirement.\n\nLevel 2 — Commercial Negotiation\nOnce the demand baseline has been controlled and optimized, procurement applies Commercial Levers to improve the economics and contractual position.\n\nThe sequence is:\nUnderstand and control demand → optimize demand/consumption → apply commercial levers → negotiate the deal.\n\nCost Management answers:\n• What are we using?\n• What are we spending?\n• Who owns the demand?\n• What should be budgeted, monitored or controlled?\n\nCost Optimization answers:\n• Can the same business need be met with less?\n• Can unused, duplicated or inefficient demand be removed?\n• Can capacity, licences, models or service levels be right-sized?\n\nCommercial Levers answer:\n• How can the optimized requirement be bought on better terms?\n• What price, discount, commitment, support, renewal, exit and data terms should be negotiated?\n\n3. Blog:\nN/A\n\n4. External:\nN/A — this is the course's procurement framework.\n\n5. Practical value:\nDo not negotiate against unmanaged demand.\nA supplier discount applied to unnecessary consumption, shelfware or oversized commitments does not create real procurement value.\n\nCloud application — slide 34:\nCost Management:\n• spend visibility by account, workload, service and region\n• budgets, alerts, forecasts and anomaly monitoring\n• tagging, cost centres, showback/chargeback\n• visibility of non-production, DR, backup and data-transfer spend\n\nCost Optimization:\n• rightsizing compute, databases and storage\n• stopping idle resources and scheduling non-production environments\n• autoscaling, serverless and storage tiering where appropriate\n• removal of orphaned resources and redesign of expensive workloads\n\nCommercial Levers:\n• commitment level and reallocation flexibility\n• discount coverage, including future services and marketplace spend where applicable\n• support and professional-service pricing\n• exit/data rights, SLA structure and hidden-cost exposure\n\nSaaS application — slide 37:\nCost Management:\n• application, licence, tier and add-on inventory\n• assigned vs active vs genuinely required users\n• spend by application, business unit and user\n• renewal calendar, utilisation and tier distribution\n\nCost Optimization:\n• reclaim inactive or duplicate licences\n• downgrade unnecessary premium tiers\n• eliminate overlapping applications and unused add-ons\n• right-size renewal quantities\n\nCommercial Levers:\n• volume bands and ramp schedules\n• price protection and renewal caps\n• true-down, transfer and reallocation rights\n• future-module discount coverage\n• support, services, exit and data portability\n\nAI application — slide 45:\nCost Management:\n• inventory use cases, users, models and workload owners\n• track token, request, compute and storage consumption\n• quotas, approval thresholds, budgets and alerts\n• team-level showback/chargeback\n\nCost Optimization:\n• match model to task complexity\n• shorten prompts and remove unnecessary context\n• cache repeated outputs and batch/reuse requests where appropriate\n• eliminate unnecessary calls before scaling\n\nCommercial Levers:\n• token/unit pricing and volume tiers\n• committed-spend flexibility across models\n• future-model, support and professional-service terms\n• data-use, ownership, IP, portability and exit rights\n\nEXAMPLES:\n• SaaS: reclaim unused licences before negotiating the renewal discount.\n• Cloud: right-size and remove idle resources before committing to a multi-year spend level.\n• AI: validate the use case and token demand before accepting a large consumption commitment.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_01 — Software Inflation; TOPIC_13 — Cloud Services; TOPIC_17 — SaaS Pricing Models; TOPIC_22 — Sourcing Value Levers\nASSOCIATED_CONCEPTS_FROM_SOURCE: Demand Management; Cost Management; Cost Optimization; Commercial Levers; Commercial Negotiation\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_28\nTOPIC_NAME: Transaction Cost Economics\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 38\n\n2. Core ideas:\nEconomic exchange occurs when two parties exchange goods, services or rights for payment or another benefit. A transaction is the transfer that puts the exchange into effect.\n\nOrganising and governing the transaction creates three groups of activities and costs:\n• Search and information — finding possible partners and verifying capability, price and quality; costs include staff time, market research, checks and evaluation.\n• Policy and contracting — agreeing scope, price, responsibilities, rules and approvals; costs include negotiation, drafting, legal review and approval effort.\n• Policing and enforcement — monitoring obligations, managing deviations and resolving disputes; costs include performance reviews, audits, dispute handling and legal action.\n\nTransaction Cost Economics compares three governance mechanisms:\n• Market / horizontal outsourcing — governed mainly through price.\n• Hybrid — governed through trust using a long-term contract or alliance.\n• Hierarchy / vertical insourcing — governed through organisational authority.\n\nThe governance choice is informed by:\n• Asset specificity — how much an investment loses value outside the relationship.\n• Uncertainty — volume, technological and behavioural uncertainty.\n• Frequency — how often the transaction occurs.\n\nThe course model associates Market with low asset specificity, low uncertainty and high frequency; Hybrid with medium asset specificity, low or medium uncertainty and medium or high frequency; and Hierarchy with high asset specificity, high uncertainty and low frequency. Where the characteristics point in different directions, TCE produces a mixed signal rather than an automatic answer.\n\n3. Blog:\nhttps://www.goodspending.com/post/why-transaction-cost-economics-belongs-in-a-sourcing-readiness-checklist\n\n4. External:\nRonald Coase — The Nature of the Firm / Nobel lecture:\nhttps://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/economic-sciences/1991/coase/lecture/\nOliver Williamson — Transaction Cost Economics / Nobel lecture:\nhttps://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/williamson_lecture.pdf\nTransaction-cost categories:\nhttps://www.coase.org/publications/2022menardshirleyadvancedintrotonie.pdf\nOverview:\nhttps://www.investopedia.com/terms/t/transactioncosts.asp\n\n5. Practical value:\n1. Define the economic exchange and the transaction.\n2. Identify the search, contracting and enforcement activities and their costs.\n3. Assess asset specificity, uncertainty and frequency.\n4. Select market, hybrid or hierarchy governance using the combined pattern—not one factor in isolation.\n5. Keep governance separate from the solution form: COTS / SaaS, customise or build.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nDropbox’s storage transaction had low or medium asset specificity, high frequency, and material technological and volume uncertainty. No case evidence establishes material behavioural uncertainty. The TCE signal was therefore mixed: market or hybrid remained plausible, and uncertainty increased the need for adaptability and control, but did not make hierarchy the automatic TCE recommendation. Dropbox’s actual insourcing decision became defensible when broader scale, internal production economics, performance, security and control factors were added.\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_14 — Make or Buy; TOPIC_25 — Incomplete Contracts; TOPIC_26 — Relational Contracts; TOPIC_29 — Property Rights Theory (PRT)\n===TOPIC END===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_29\nTOPIC_NAME: Property Rights Theory (PRT)\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 38\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES: None\n\n2. Core ideas:\nPlain-language question: who should own the assets used or created in a supplier relationship, and how does that encourage investment?\nProperty Rights Theory is associated with Sanford Grossman, Oliver Hart and John Moore.\nOwnership includes residual control rights: decisions about an asset's use that the contract has not already assigned.\nOwnership affects each party's alternatives if negotiations fail. Parties anticipate those alternatives when deciding how much extra effort to invest beforehand.\nThe relevant investments include learning, initiative and improvement that a contract cannot fully specify, verify and reward. Paying agreed fees is compensation, not itself evidence of non-contractible investment.\nStronger control can encourage one party's investment while weakening the other's. Customer ownership is not automatically best, and ownership does not guarantee practical independence.\nReuse can provide a supplier with returns from other customers. It is one consideration, not a complete rule for deciding ownership.\n\nComparison with Transaction Cost Economics:\nBoth theories help explain integration and firm boundaries. They do not offer mutually exclusive solutions called integration versus ownership. In PRT, integration brings assets under common ownership.\nPractical TCE application in this course: should SAP support remain internal or be outsourced? Compare coordination, adaptation and safeguarding costs under market, hybrid and internal governance.\nPractical PRT application: who should own support tools, and how does that affect the willingness of both parties to invest?\nWilliamson's TCE uses bounded rationality and possible opportunism. Standard Grossman–Hart–Moore PRT can assume fully rational parties, but important investments cannot be verified and enforced through the contract. Do not describe bounded rationality as a shared required assumption.\nThese are teaching applications, not exclusive theoretical boundaries. PRT alone does not select Staff Augmentation, IT Outsourcing, Managed Services or As-a-Service.\n\n5. Practical value:\nIdentify the critical additional investment from each party.\nAssess its usefulness outside the relationship.\nAsk whether owning or controlling the relevant asset changes a usable alternative and willingness to invest.\nCompare the incentive effects on both parties before allocating ownership.\nDistinguish supplier reusable tools from customer-specific deliverables and establish usable customer rights.\n\nEXAMPLES:\nIllustrative SAP support example, not a documented company case:\nA support supplier develops automation. Supplier ownership and reuse elsewhere can reward further development. The customer should establish usage and continuity rights for any tools on which operations depend.\nCustomer ownership of bespoke automation can support further customer investment and control. The parties still need to consider how to fund and reward the supplier's additional development effort.\nIn the user's SAP case, integration tools are paid upfront while learning and other investment recover through support fees. Funding alone does not establish the ownership rights acquired or the incentive for further improvement.\nDiscussion: would demanding exclusive customer ownership encourage or discourage the improvements you want?\n\n4. External:\nhttps://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/hart-lecture.pdf\nhttps://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/advanced-economicsciences2016-1.pdf\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_28 — Transaction Cost Economics; TOPIC_14 — Make or Buy; TOPIC_25 — Incomplete Contracts; TOPIC_26 — Relational Contracts\n===TOPIC END===\n\n=== TOPIC BLOCKS END ===\n\n===TOPIC START===\nTOPIC_ID: TOPIC_30\nTOPIC_NAME: Outcome-based pricing\nCURRENT_TEACHING_SLIDES: 46\nRELATED_ACTIVITY_OR_ROUTING_SLIDES: 47\n\nCore ideas:\nOutcome-based pricing is a subset of value-based pricing. Value-based pricing sets price using customer value. Outcome-based pricing makes the outcome-linked fee conditional on a defined, verified result.\nA subscription can be value-based without making payment conditional on achieved results.\nUsage-based pricing charges for consumption such as tokens, API calls or transactions, whether or not the intended outcome is achieved.\nOutcome-linked fees may coexist with a base subscription, onboarding fee or minimum commitment. Describe these as billing components, not as combining two separate outcome-based and value-based models.\nA resolved support case is an illustrative chargeable outcome only when the parties define success, quality, exclusions and verification.\n\nProcurement controls:\nDefine the chargeable success event and eligible scope. Specify failure, reopen, escalation and duplicate rules.\nAgree attribution, measurement window, data source, audit rights and dispute resolution.\nModel spend at realistic volumes. Negotiate base fees, minimums, caps, overages and price changes.\nCheck service quality and business outcomes, not just chargeable event counts.\nAllocate responsibility for buyer-side dependencies; avoid rewarding easily counted events that do not deliver the intended value.\n\nIllustrative example:\nPay a fee for each verified support resolution that meets agreed quality criteria and remains closed during an agreed measurement window. No price or window length is prescribed.\n\nSources:\nhttps://www.zendesk.com/blog/ai/agentic-ai/outcome-based-pricing/\nhttps://www.thesaascfo.com/how-to-build-outcome-based-pricing/\nhttps://stripe.com/resources/more/cost-based-and-value-based-pricing\n\nRELATED_TOPICS: TOPIC_17 — SaaS Pricing Models; TOPIC_03 — SaaS Market Reality & Trends; TOPIC_26 — Relational Contracts\n===TOPIC END===\n";

const APP_HTML = "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"><title>IT Procurement Foundations Course Refresher</title>\n<style>\n:root{--navy:#102a43;--blue:#1463a5;--pale:#eef5fb;--line:#d7e2ec;--text:#243b53;--muted:#627d98;--white:#fff}*{box-sizing:border-box}body{margin:0;background:#f4f7fa;color:var(--text);font:16px/1.5 system-ui,-apple-system,\"Segoe UI\",sans-serif}.shell{max-width:1040px;margin:0 auto;padding:28px 20px 40px}.head{background:var(--navy);color:#fff;padding:26px 28px;border-radius:16px 16px 0 0}.eyebrow{text-transform:uppercase;font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.12em;color:#b9d7ee;font-weight:700}.head h1{margin:5px 0 4px;font-size:clamp(24px,4vw,34px);line-height:1.15}.head p{margin:8px 0 0;color:#e4eff8;max-width:700px}.panel{background:#fff;padding:22px 24px;border-radius:0 0 16px 16px;box-shadow:0 10px 30px #102a4310}.notice{border-left:4px solid #72a7d4;background:var(--pale);padding:10px 13px;margin:0 0 18px;font-size:14px}.row{display:flex;gap:10px;align-items:end}.field{flex:1}.field label{display:block;font-weight:650;margin:0 0 6px}.field input,.search{width:100%;border:1px solid #bcccdc;border-radius:8px;padding:11px 12px;font:inherit;color:var(--text);background:#fff}.primary{border:0;border-radius:8px;background:var(--blue);color:#fff;font:inherit;font-weight:700;padding:11px 17px;cursor:pointer}.primary:hover{background:#0d5189}.section-title{margin:26px 0 5px;color:var(--navy);font-size:22px}.helper{margin:0 0 14px;color:var(--muted)}.search{max-width:440px}.topic-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(230px,1fr));gap:10px;margin-top:14px}.topic-card{display:block;width:100%;min-height:64px;text-align:left;border:1px solid var(--line);background:#fff;border-radius:10px;padding:13px 14px;color:var(--text);font:inherit;cursor:pointer;transition:background .15s,border-color .15s,transform .15s}.topic-card:hover,.topic-card:focus-visible{border-color:#70a6d2;background:#f2f8fd;transform:translateY(-1px);outline:2px solid #70a6d2;outline-offset:1px}.topic-card strong{display:block;color:var(--navy);font-size:15px}.topic-card span{font-size:12px;color:var(--muted)}.conversation{margin-top:20px;display:grid;gap:14px}.bubble{border-radius:12px;padding:14px 16px;max-width:94%;white-space:pre-wrap}.user{background:#e8f1f8;margin-left:auto}.assistant{background:#f2f5f8;border:1px solid #e0e7ed}.related{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;margin-top:10px}.related-title{font-weight:700;margin-top:16px}.related button{border:1px solid #a8c7de;background:#eef5fb;color:#102a43;border-radius:999px;padding:7px 12px;cursor:pointer;font:inherit}.related button:hover,.related button:focus-visible{background:#dcecf8;outline:2px solid #1463a5}.concept-detail{margin-top:10px;padding:12px;background:#fff;border-left:3px solid #1463a5}.loading{color:var(--muted);font-style:italic}.error{color:#a12c2c}.footer{padding:14px 2px;color:var(--muted);font-size:12px}@media(max-width:600px){.shell{padding:12px}.head{padding:21px 18px}.panel{padding:18px 15px}.row{align-items:stretch;flex-direction:column}.bubble{max-width:100%}}\n\n.course-resources{margin-top:28px;padding-top:22px;border-top:1px solid var(--line)}.resource-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,230px),1fr));gap:10px;margin-top:14px}.resource-link{display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:10px;min-height:64px;padding:13px 14px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;color:var(--navy);background:var(--pale);font-weight:700;text-decoration:none;overflow-wrap:anywhere}.resource-link:hover,.resource-link:focus-visible{background:#dcecf8;border-color:#70a6d2;outline:2px solid var(--blue);outline-offset:2px}.resource-link span[aria-hidden]{flex:none;color:var(--blue)}\n</style></head><body><main class=\"shell\"><header class=\"head\"><div class=\"eyebrow\">Good Spending · Learning resource</div><h1>IT Procurement Foundations Course Refresher</h1><p>Refresh course concepts with concise explanations grounded in the course material.</p></header><section class=\"panel\">\n<div class=\"notice\">Choose a topic for a refresher, or ask a question about the course.</div>\n<form id=\"ask-form\"><div class=\"row\"><div class=\"field\"><label for=\"question\">Ask a course question</label><input id=\"question\" maxlength=\"1200\" placeholder=\"e.g., How does the Buygrid Framework affect sourcing effort?\" required></div><button class=\"primary\" type=\"submit\">Ask</button></div></form>\n<div id=\"thread\" class=\"conversation\" aria-live=\"polite\"></div>\n<h2 class=\"section-title\">Course topics</h2><p class=\"helper\">Select a topic to open its refresher. Filter by keyword if needed.</p><input id=\"topic-search\" class=\"search\" placeholder=\"Search topics…\" aria-label=\"Search course topics\"><div id=\"topic-grid\" class=\"topic-grid\"></div>\n<nav class=\"course-resources\" aria-labelledby=\"course-resources-title\"><h2 id=\"course-resources-title\" class=\"section-title\">Course resources &amp; practical tools</h2><p class=\"helper\">Open a course resource or apply the concepts with a practical tool. Links open in a new tab.</p><div class=\"resource-grid\"><a class=\"resource-link\" href=\"https://www.goodspending.com/it-procurement-negotiation-simulator\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Negotiation Simulator<span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a class=\"resource-link\" href=\"https://www.goodspending.com/sourcing-strategy-tool\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">IT Sourcing Strategy Tool<span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a class=\"resource-link\" href=\"https://www.goodspending.com/kraljic-matrix-demo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Kraljic Matrix Demo Tool<span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a class=\"resource-link\" href=\"https://www.goodspending.com/my-courses/sourcing-readiness-checklist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sourcing Readiness Checklist<span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a><a class=\"resource-link\" href=\"https://www.goodspending.com/my-courses/itservicemodelselector\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">IT Service Model Selector<span aria-hidden=\"true\">↗</span></a></div></nav>\n</section><div class=\"footer\">Responses are limited to the supplied course material.</div></main>\n<script>\nconst topics=[{\"id\": \"TOPIC_01\", \"name\": \"Software Inflation\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_02\", \"name\": \"Digitalization Paradox\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_03\", \"name\": \"SaaS Market Reality & Trends\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_04\", \"name\": \"Servitization of IT\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_05\", \"name\": \"Digitization vs Digitalization\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_06\", \"name\": \"Bimodal IT\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_07\", \"name\": \"Buygrid Framework\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_08\", \"name\": \"Pace Layer Model\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_09\", \"name\": \"ITIL Service Lifecycle\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_10\", \"name\": \"TCO of a Technology Service\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_11\", \"name\": \"NIST Cloud Computing Model\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_12\", \"name\": \"Cloud Deployment Models\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_13\", \"name\": \"Cloud Services\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_14\", \"name\": \"Make or Buy\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_15\", \"name\": \"Horizontal, Vertical, and Nano-SaaS\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_16\", \"name\": \"The SaaSpocalypse\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_17\", \"name\": \"SaaS Pricing Models\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_18\", \"name\": \"Types of IT Outsourcing Services\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_19\", \"name\": \"Outsourcing Delivery Models\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_20\", \"name\": \"ISO 19770-1:2017 Framework of ITAM Processes\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_21\", \"name\": \"Kraljic Matrix\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_22\", \"name\": \"Sourcing Value Levers\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_23\", \"name\": \"Information Asymmetry\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_24\", \"name\": \"Types of Negotiations and Game Theory\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_25\", \"name\": \"Incomplete Contracts\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_26\", \"name\": \"Relational Contracts\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_27\", \"name\": \"Technology Procurement Framework\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_28\", \"name\": \"Transaction Cost Economics\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_29\", \"name\": \"Property Rights Theory (PRT)\"}, {\"id\": \"TOPIC_30\", \"name\": \"Outcome-based pricing\"}];\nconst endpoint=location.origin;\nfunction safe(s){return String(s).replace(/[&<>\"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','\"':'&quot;',\"'\":'&#39;'}[c]));}\nconst thread=document.getElementById('thread');\nfunction addBubble(cls,text){const el=document.createElement('div');el.className='bubble '+cls;el.textContent=text;thread.appendChild(el);el.scrollIntoView({block:'nearest'});return el;}\nasync function requestAnswer(payload,displayText){thread.replaceChildren();addBubble('user',displayText);const wait=addBubble('assistant loading','Looking in the course material…');try{const r=await fetch(endpoint+'/answer',{method:'POST',headers:{'Content-Type':'application/json'},body:JSON.stringify(payload)});const d=await r.json();if(!r.ok||!d.success)throw new Error(d.error||'Could not get a course refresher.');wait.classList.remove('loading');wait.textContent=d.answer;\nif(Array.isArray(d.related)&&d.related.length){const title=document.createElement('div');title.className='related-title';title.textContent='Related course concepts';wait.appendChild(title);const links=document.createElement('div');links.className='related';wait.appendChild(links);for(const item of d.related){const b=document.createElement('button');b.type='button';b.textContent=item.name;if(item.type==='topic'){b.addEventListener('click',()=>requestAnswer({mode:'topic',topicId:item.id},item.name));}else{b.setAttribute('aria-expanded','false');b.addEventListener('click',()=>{const prior=wait.querySelector('.concept-detail');if(prior){prior.remove();b.setAttribute('aria-expanded','false');return;}const detail=document.createElement('div');detail.className='concept-detail';detail.textContent=item.detail;wait.appendChild(detail);b.setAttribute('aria-expanded','true');});}links.appendChild(b);}}}catch(e){wait.classList.remove('loading');wait.classList.add('error');wait.textContent=e.message;}}\ndocument.getElementById('ask-form').addEventListener('submit',e=>{e.preventDefault();const q=document.getElementById('question').value.trim();if(q)requestAnswer({mode:'ask',question:q},q);});\nconst grid=document.getElementById('topic-grid');\nfunction renderTopics(filter=''){grid.replaceChildren();const match=topics.filter(t=>(t.name+' '+t.id).toLowerCase().includes(filter.toLowerCase()));if(!match.length){const p=document.createElement('p');p.className='helper';p.textContent='No matching topics.';grid.appendChild(p);return;}for(const t of match){const b=document.createElement('button');b.type='button';b.className='topic-card';b.setAttribute('aria-label','Open refresher: '+t.name);b.innerHTML='<strong>'+safe(t.name)+'</strong><span>Select to read the refresher</span>';b.addEventListener('click',()=>requestAnswer({mode:'topic',topicId:t.id},t.name));grid.appendChild(b);}}\nrenderTopics();document.getElementById('topic-search').addEventListener('input',e=>renderTopics(e.target.value));\n</script></body></html>";

const SOURCE_NOTE = "80-slide ITPF deck, 20 October 2026. Outcome-based pricing added as TOPIC_30. New slide numbering throughout.";

const TRUSTED_ORIGINS = new Set(["https://www.goodspending.com","https://goodspending.com"]);

function parseTopics(){const out={};for(const m of KNOWLEDGE.matchAll(/===TOPIC START===\s*([\s\S]*?)\s*===TOPIC END===/g)){const content=m[1];const id=content.match(/^TOPIC_ID:\s*(.+)$/m)?.[1]?.trim();const name=content.match(/^TOPIC_NAME:\s*(.+)$/m)?.[1]?.trim();if(id&&name)out[id]={id,name,content};}return out;}

const TOPICS=parseTopics();

const STOP=new Set("a an and are as at be been being by can could did do does for from had has have how i in into is it its may might of on or our should than that the their them then there these they this to was we were what when where which who why will with would you your about explain tell please course procurement topic use using apply applied scenario concept concepts".split(/\s+/));

function tokens(s){return (String(s).toLowerCase().match(/[a-z0-9]+/g)||[]).filter(t=>t.length>2&&!STOP.has(t));}

function score(q,text){const terms=new Set(tokens(q));const body=String(text).toLowerCase();let n=0;for(const t of terms){const re=new RegExp("\\b"+t+"\\b","g");n+=Math.min((body.match(re)||[]).length,4);}return n;}

function corsHeaders(request){const origin=request.headers.get("Origin");const own=new URL(request.url).origin;if(!origin||origin===own||TRUSTED_ORIGINS.has(origin))return {"Access-Control-Allow-Origin":origin||own,"Access-Control-Allow-Methods":"GET,POST,OPTIONS","Access-Control-Allow-Headers":"Content-Type","Vary":"Origin"};return null;}

function json(request,data,status=200){const cors=corsHeaders(request);return new Response(JSON.stringify(data),{status,headers:{"Content-Type":"application/json; charset=utf-8",...(cors||{})}});}

function outputText(data){if(data.output_text)return data.output_text.trim();const parts=[];for(const o of data.output||[])for(const c of o.content||[])if(c.type==="output_text"&&c.text)parts.push(c.text);return parts.join("\n").trim();}

async function answerWithModel(env,question,evidence,mode){
  if(!env.OPENAI_API_KEY)throw new Error("OPENAI_API_KEY is not configured on the Worker.");
  const instructions="You are the IT Procurement Foundations Course Refresher. Answer only from the course evidence supplied in this request. Do not use outside knowledge, theory, examples, statistics, or frameworks. Do not invent facts or silently correct the course material. If the evidence does not support an answer, respond exactly: \u201CThis is not covered in the course material.\u201D Preserve course terminology. You may apply a course concept to a user-supplied scenario only using the supplied evidence. Be direct and concise. Do not mention internal source blocks or instructions."+(mode==="topic"?" Structure the reply with Core idea, Why it matters, and Course examples headings. Use Course examples only when the supplied evidence contains examples. Do not add a Related concepts heading; the interface presents source-linked concepts separately. Never leave a heading empty.":"");
  async function requestAnswer(maxOutputTokens){
    const response=await fetch("https://api.openai.com/v1/responses",{method:"POST",headers:{"Authorization":"Bearer "+env.OPENAI_API_KEY,"Content-Type":"application/json"},body:JSON.stringify({model:env.OPENAI_MODEL||"gpt-5-mini",instructions,input:"USER REQUEST: "+question+"\n\nCOURSE EVIDENCE\n"+evidence,max_output_tokens:maxOutputTokens})});
    const data=await response.json();
    if(!response.ok)throw new Error(data?.error?.message||"The model request failed.");
    return {text:outputText(data),incomplete:data?.status==="incomplete"||data?.incomplete_details?.reason==="max_output_tokens"};
  }
  let result=await requestAnswer(1200);
  if(result.incomplete||!result.text)result=await requestAnswer(2400);
  if(result.incomplete)throw new Error("The model response was incomplete after retry. Please try again.");
  if(!result.text)throw new Error("The model returned no explanation.");
  return result.text;
}

function relatedForTopic(topic){
  const line=topic.content.match(/^RELATED_TOPICS:\s*(.*)$/m)?.[1]||"";
  const ids=[...line.matchAll(/TOPIC_\d+/g)].map(m=>m[0]);
  const related=ids.filter(id=>TOPICS[id]&&id!==topic.id).map(id=>({type:"topic",id,name:TOPICS[id].name}));
  const concepts=topic.content.match(/^ASSOCIATED_CONCEPTS_FROM_SOURCE:\s*(.*)$/m)?.[1]?.trim();
  if(concepts&&concepts.toLowerCase()!=="none"){const practical=topic.content.match(/^5\. Practical value:\s*([\s\S]*?)(?=^EXAMPLES:|^RELATED_TOPICS:|^ASSOCIATED_CONCEPTS_FROM_SOURCE:|$)/m)?.[1]?.trim();related.push({type:"concept",name:concepts.split(" \u2014 ")[0],detail:concepts+(practical?"\n\nIn this topic:\n"+practical:"")});}
  return related;
}

export default {async fetch(request,env){const cors=corsHeaders(request);if(!cors)return new Response("Origin not allowed",{status:403});if(request.method==="OPTIONS")return new Response(null,{status:204,headers:cors});const url=new URL(request.url);if(request.method==="GET"&&url.pathname==="/")return new Response(APP_HTML,{headers:{"Content-Type":"text/html; charset=utf-8","X-Content-Type-Options":"nosniff",...cors}});if(request.method==="GET"&&url.pathname==="/health")return json(request,{success:true,service:"itpf-course-refresher",topicCount:Object.keys(TOPICS).length,sourceNote:SOURCE_NOTE});if(request.method==="GET"&&url.pathname==="/topics")return json(request,{success:true,topics:Object.values(TOPICS).map(({id,name})=>({id,name}))});if(request.method!=="POST"||url.pathname!=="/answer")return json(request,{success:false,error:"Not found."},404);let payload;try{payload=await request.json();}catch{return json(request,{success:false,error:"Invalid JSON body."},400);}const mode=String(payload.mode||"ask");let question="",evidence="";if(mode==="topic"){const t=TOPICS[String(payload.topicId||"")];if(!t)return json(request,{success:false,error:"Select a valid course topic."},400);question="Explain the course topic: "+t.name;evidence=t.content;}else if(mode==="ask"){question=String(payload.question||"").trim();if(!question||question.length>1200)return json(request,{success:false,error:"Enter a question of up to 1,200 characters."},400);if(/^\s*(list|topics|contents)\s*[?.!]*\s*$/i.test(question))return json(request,{success:true,answer:Object.values(TOPICS).map(t=>t.id+" \u2014 "+t.name).join("\n")});const ranked=Object.values(TOPICS).map(t=>({t,s:score(question,t.name+" "+t.content)})).sort((a,b)=>b.s-a.s).slice(0,3).filter(x=>x.s>0);if(!ranked.length)return json(request,{success:true,answer:"This is not covered in the course material."});evidence=ranked.map(x=>x.t.content).join("\n\n---\n\n");}else return json(request,{success:false,error:"mode must be ask or topic."},400);try{return json(request,{success:true,answer:await answerWithModel(env,question,evidence,mode),related:mode==="topic"?relatedForTopic(TOPICS[String(payload.topicId)]):[]});}catch(e){return json(request,{success:false,error:e.message},502);}}};
